 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the agriculture and markets law, in relation to import of\ndairy products and payment from the milk producers security fund",
    "basePrintNo": "S6714",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6714-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§71-n & 258-b, Ag & Mkts L",
    "lawSection": "Agriculture and Markets Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6714\n\nSPONSOR: HINCHEY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the agriculture and markets law, in relation to import\nof dairy products and payment from the milk producers security fund\n\n1.   \nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of that part of the bill that would amend AML section 71-n\n(1) is to repeal the requirement that a person who imports milk and milk\nproducts into the State must obtain a permit from the Commissioner of\nAgriculture and Markets (\"Commissioner\") to do so.\n\nThe purpose of that part of the bill that would amend AML section 258-b\nis to allow the Commissioner to make a partial payment to a dairy farmer\nwho has not been paid for milk, if that dairy farmer has made a claim\nagainst alternative security filed by the milk dealer who has defaulted\nin payment to such producer.\n\n2.   \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends AML section 71-n (1) to delete the require-\nment that a person who imports milk and milk products into the State\nmust obtain a permit prior to doing so.\n\nSection 2 of the bill amends A&ML section 258-b to authorize the Commis-\nsioner to make a partial payment to a dairy farmer who has sold milk to\na milk dealer who has not made payment therefor and has filed alterna-\ntive security rather than participating in such fund.\n\n3.   \nEXISTING LAW:\n\nAML section 71-n(1) currently requires a person who imports milk and\nmilk products into the State to obtain a permit from the Commissioner\nprior to doing so.\n\nAML section 258-b currently authorizes the Commissioner to advance 75%\nof a dairy farmer's claim for unpaid milk if the defaulting milk dealer\nis participating in the milk producers security fund.\n\n4.   \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNone.\n\n5.   \nSTATEMENT IN SUPPORT:\n\nThe proposed amendment to AML section 71-n (1) deletes the requirement\nthat a person may not import milk and milk products into the State\nunless granted a permit to do so; this requirement imposes an unneces-\nsary burden upon interstate commerce since imported milk and milk\nproducts are currently, and will continue to be, required to meet all\nstandards and requirements applicable to milk and milk products produced\nor manufactured in this State.\n\nThe proposed amendment to AML section 71-n (1) will benefit the State's\nconsumers in that they very well could have access to a greater variety\nof milk and milk products. In addition, the proposed amendment may bene-\nfit the State's dairy farmers because many of them sell milk to process-\nors and manufacturers located out-of-state that sell and distribute milk\nand milk products in the State; the demand for the milk produced by such\ndairy farmers may increase if importation is made less burdensome.\n\nThe proposed amendments to AML section 258-b would allow the Commission-\ner to advance 50% of a dairy farmers claim for unpaid milk if the\ndefaulting dealer has secured its milk purchases by providing alterna-\ntive security. As set forth above, such advance payment can presently be\nmade only if the defaulting milk dealer has secured its purchases by\npaying into the milk producers security fund. There is little reason why\na dairy farmer who has not been paid for milk sold and delivered should\nbe eligible to receive an advance payment while another dairy farmer,\nsimilarly situated, should not be merely because the latter sold milk to\na milk dealer who has not participated in the milk producers security\nfund; this amendment will correct this inequity and may enable certain\ndairy farmer to stay in business who, under current law, might not be\nable to do SO.\n\n6.   \nPOSSIBLE OBJECTIONS:\n\nThe proposed amendments to AML section 71-n (1) repeal an unnecessary\nstatutory mandate; as such, no reasonable objections are anticipated.\n\nThe proposed amendments to AML section 258-b will allow the Commissioner\nto obtain up to 50% of the estimated value of a claim against a default-\ning dealer and to obtain these funds from the Letter of Credit or Bond\nthat was provided as alternate security to pay dairy farmers who have\nsold milk to defaulting dealers who have filed alternative security. It\nis possible that this may increase the cost of obtaining this security.\n\n7.   \nOTHER STATE AGENCIES INTERESTED:\n\nNone\n\n8.   \nKNOWN POSITION OF OTHERS:\n\nUnknown.\n\n9.   \nBUDGET IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n10.   \nLOCAL IMPACT:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
